sentences.l:
If with you I-escape sound or alive,
still soon or late the king to-want me-will as friend;
if not, as-much-as I-leave not it I-prize a fig.”

sentences.t:
Con vuestro consejo bastir quiero dos arcas;
inchámoslas d'arena, ca bien serán pesadas,
cubiertas de guadalmeçí e bien enclaveadas.

sentences.l:
With your counsel to-build I-want two chests;
let-us-fill-them with sand, for well they-will-be heavy,
covered with gilded-leather and well nailed.

sentences.t:
“Raquel e Vidas, amos me dat las manos,
que non me descubrades a moros nin a cristianos;
por siempre vos faré ricos, que non seades menguados.

sentences.l:
“Raquel and Vidas, both to-me give the hands,
that not me you-uncover to Moors nor to Christians;
for ever you I-shall-make rich, that not you-may-be in-want.

sentences.t:
Prended las arcas e metedlas en vuestro salvo;
con grand jura meted i las fe[de]s amos,
que non las catedes en todo aqueste año.”

sentences.l:
Take the chests and put-them in your safe-keeping;
with great oath put therein the faiths both (of you),
that not them you-look-into in all this year.”

sentences.t:
Respuso Martín Antolínez a guisa de menbrado:
“myo Çid querrá, lo que ssea aguisado;
pedir vos a poco por dexar so aver en salvo.

sentences.l:
Answered Martín Antolínez in the manner of a shrewd-man:
“my Çid will-want that which may-be fitting;
to-ask you he-will little for leaving his wealth in safety.

sentences.t:
Martín Antolínez el pleyto a parado,
que sobre aquellas arcas dar le ien seysçientos marcos,
e bien gelas guardarien fasta cabo del año;

sentences.l:
Martín Antolínez the agreement has settled,
that upon those chests to-give to-him they-would six-hundred marks,
and well them-for-him they-would-guard until end of-the year;

for thus-to-him they-had-given the faith and it-to-him they-had sworn,
that if before them they-should-look-into, that they-should-be forsworn,
not to-them should-give my Çid of gain a penny bad.
